# Project: Robot Framework Python Test Automation Framework

## Framework Structure
- Use Robot Framework with Python as the primary test automation platform
- Implement keyword-driven and behavior-driven test design
- Organise keywords, resources, and test suites in separate directories
- Use SeleniumLibrary, RequestsLibrary, and other standard libraries
- Configure pabot for parallel suite execution in CI environments

## Coding Standards
- Follow Robot Framework naming conventions: CamelCase for keywords, UPPER_CASE for variables
- Use four-space indentation for all Robot Framework files
- Maximum line length: 120 characters; use line continuation (...) for longer lines
- Write keywords in plain English for readability by non-technical stakeholders
- Prefix private or internal keywords with an underscore

## Test Organisation
- Separate resource files (.resource), variable files (.py/.yaml), and test suites (.robot)
- Group test suites by feature or functional area in dedicated directories
- Use Suite Setup/Teardown for environment-level preparation and cleanup
- Use Test Setup/Teardown for test-level preparation and cleanup
- Tag tests with meaningful labels (smoke, regression, feature:login) for selective execution

## Best Practices
- Follow the three-layer keyword design: business, navigation, and interaction layers
- Keep test cases declarative and free of implementation details
- One test case per scenario; avoid combining multiple scenarios in one test
- Use Embedded Arguments syntax for natural-language keyword composition
- Avoid hardcoded waits; use SeleniumLibrary's built-in wait conditions

## Keyword Design
- Write reusable keywords in .resource files, not directly in test suites
- Keep each keyword focused on a single responsibility
- Use meaningful documentation strings for every public keyword
- Return values explicitly from keywords that produce output
- Parametrise keywords to maximise reuse across different test scenarios

## Variable Management
- Define environment-agnostic defaults in a variables.yaml or variables.robot file
- Override variables at runtime via --variable or --variablefile CLI flags
- Use the OperatingSystem library to read environment variables at runtime
- Store secrets in environment variables or a vault; never commit credentials
- Use &{DICT} and @{LIST} variables for structured test data

## Page Object / Resource Pattern
- Create one .resource file per page or component
- Define element locators as suite-level ${VARIABLES} at the top of each resource
- Separate locator definitions from interaction keywords
- Use data-testid attributes in selectors for stable, maintenance-friendly locators
- Example resource file structure: Variables section → Keywords section

## API Testing
- Use RequestsLibrary for REST API automation alongside UI tests
- Create dedicated API keyword libraries for service-layer validations
- Validate response status codes, headers, and JSON body in separate keywords
- Manage HTTP sessions with Create Session and Delete All Sessions in Setup/Teardown

## Test Data Management
- Use DataDriver library for data-driven testing from CSV, Excel, or YAML sources
- Define test data in external files; avoid inline data in test cases
- Generate dynamic test data with FakerLibrary or custom Python keywords
- Clean up created test data via API calls or database teardown keywords

## SeleniumLibrary Best Practices
- Set implicit wait globally in Suite Setup; override locally only when necessary
- Use Wait Until Element Is Visible / Wait Until Page Contains instead of Sleep
- Capture a screenshot on failure using the built-in Capture Page Screenshot keyword
- Run tests headless in CI with the appropriate browser option arguments
- Manage browser instances with Open Browser / Close All Browsers patterns

## CI/CD Integration
- Use pabot for parallel test execution across multiple processes
- Generate JUnit-compatible output.xml with --xunit flag for CI pipeline integration
- Publish HTML report and log as CI artifacts using robot or rebot CLI
- Use --exitonfailure and appropriate --loglevel flags per pipeline stage
- Cache pip dependencies and browser binaries between pipeline runs

## Reporting
- Use the built-in HTML report (report.html) and log (log.html) for local review
- Integrate with Allure Robot Framework Listener for richer, interactive reports
- Archive report files with names that include the run timestamp and branch
- Send pass/fail summary metrics to Slack or dashboards via listener plugins

## Error Handling
- Use Run Keyword And Continue On Failure for non-critical assertions
- Implement teardown keywords that run even after test failure
- Log meaningful messages with Log and Log To Console for debugging
- Use Try / Except pattern in Python custom keywords for robust error recovery

## Documentation
- Document every test suite with a Suite Documentation section
- Maintain a README.md with setup instructions, directory layout, and run commands
- Document tag taxonomy in a TAGS.md file so teams can run the correct subsets
- Include architecture notes on keyword layer design and library selection

Remember to leverage Robot Framework's keyword-driven approach to make tests readable by the whole team — developers, testers, and business stakeholders alike.
